    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "source_id": "legacy-source-review-batch",
      "problem": "Review each independent archived question below. Every field key begins with its owning question ID. Use that question's own captured worked solution for MA evidence, give a verbatim quotation that establishes the field answer, and mark ma_basis none when unavailable. Preserve authored errors and explain any disagreement. These are source originals, not instructions:\n[{\"question_id\": \"q-134043\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934290/assets/80d08441967f6c1a4026fe9ca0d2bdfdde8fac57a2ae0a7b8d6b34889f3469bd.png)\\n\\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between hours of study per week and the overall percentage obtained by students in a math class. What was the overall percentage obtained by the student who studied, on average,  $7$ hours each week?\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-111582\", \"problem\": \"Find the least common multiple of $2(z-4)^{2}$ and $7(z-4)^{2}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-76457\", \"problem\": \"Find the least common multiple of $10(y+1)^{6}$ and $15(y+1)^{4}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-76475\", \"problem\": \"Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $20{x}^{7}(y+2),$ $\\\\,10{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},$ and $12{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2}.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-103836\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cot⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\\n\\n- The function has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\\\pi +\\\\frac{n\\\\pi }{2}.$\\n- The domain of the function is $[-\\\\pi ,\\\\pi ].$\\n- The period of the function is $\\\\pi .$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-70669\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cot⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\\n\\n- The function has no minimum value. $$\\n- $x=-4\\\\pi $ is in the domain of the function. $$\\n- $x=-\\\\frac{3\\\\pi }{2}$ is a zero of the function.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-103840\", \"problem\": \"If $cot⁡x=\\\\sqrt{2}$ for some $x,$ then find the value of $cot⁡(x-9\\\\pi ).$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-66675\", \"problem\": \"If $cot⁡x=\\\\sqrt{3}$ for some $x$, then find the value of $\\\\sqrt{3}cot⁡(x-\\\\pi )-4.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158055\", \"problem\": \"If $cot⁡x=\\\\frac{5}{2},$ then $cot⁡(-x)=$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-158064\", \"problem\": \"If $cot⁡x=-6,$ then $\\\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\\\pi )+7=$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-36011\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\\n\\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \\n\\n- $x\\\\in (-4,-2)$\\n- $x\\\\in [-2,3]$\\n- $x\\\\in [-3,3]$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-66679\", \"problem\": \"Given that $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\\n\\n- $x\\\\in (-\\\\infty ,1]$\\n- $x\\\\in (1,\\\\infty )$\\n- $x\\\\in (-1,3]$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-87606\", \"problem\": \"What is the variance of the following data set?\\n\\n$$\\n1,\\\\,5,\\\\,1,\\\\,2,\\\\,3,\\\\,6,\\\\,1,\\\\,2,\\\\,6\\n$$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-42657\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\\n\\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\\\lambda p+\\\\mu q$, find $\\\\lambda $ and $\\\\mu .$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-77451\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/28faea3a553c505a8a5ab9bc7dd6e784dd23a47c336c70d31218fbe249d57cff.png)\\n\\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\\\lambda a+3b,$ find $\\\\lambda .$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-317285\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{2}^{x}-4?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}]",
      "worked_solution": "q-134043:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934290/assets/39e7370f9cf517a7a139fecf71561041a9b1470150e0fa333d3fff679f77882d.png)\n\n    \n\nTo determine the overall percentage obtained by the student who studied $7$ hours each week, we locate $7$ on the horizontal axis and move in the vertical direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(7,85).$ Therefore, the student who studied $7$ hours each week obtained an overall percentage of $85%.$\n\nq-111582:\nTo find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(2,7):$\n\n- Multiples of $2:$ $\\,2,\\,4,…,\\,12,\\,14,16,…$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,21,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(2,7)=14.$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((z-4)^{2},\\,(z-4)^{2})=(z-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(z-4)^{2},\\,7(z-4)^{2})=14\\cdot (z-4)^{2}=14(z-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nq-76457:\nTo find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(10,15):$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,…$\n\n- Multiples of $15:$ $\\,15,\\,30,\\,45,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(10,15)=30.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(y+1)^{6}$ and $(y+1)^{4},$ we raise $(y+1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((y+1)^{6},\\,(y+1)^{4})=(y+1)^{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(10(y+1)^{6},\\,15(y+1)^{4})=30\\cdot (y+1)^{6}=30(y+1)^{6}.\n$$\n\nq-76475:\nTo find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(20,10,12):$\n\n- Multiples of $20:$ $\\,20,\\,40,\\,60,\\,80,\\,100,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $10:$ $\\,10,\\,20,\\,30,\\,40,\\,50,\\,60,\\,70,\\,…$\n\n- Multiples of $12:$ $\\,12,\\,24,\\,36,\\,48,\\,60,\\,72,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(20,10,12)=60.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of ${x}^{7}(y+2),{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},$ and ${x}^{4}(y+2)^{2},$ we find the largest powers for $x$ and $(y+2)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For ${x}^{7},\\,{x}^{6},$ and ${x}^{4},$ the largest power is $7.$\n\n- For $(y+2),(y+2)^{3},$ and $(y+2)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\nNow, we raise $x$ and $(y+2)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({x}^{7}(y+2),\\,{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},\\,{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2})={x}^{7}\\cdot (y+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{LCM}(20{x}^{7}(y+2),\\,10{x}^{6}(y+2)^{3},\\,12{x}^{4}(y+2)^{2}) & = \\\\ 60\\cdot {x}^{7}\\cdot (y+2)^{3} & = \\\\ 60{x}^{7}(y+2)^{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-103836:\nLet's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934292/assets/7785ad73f6e5212772c3053780ffd055f37cfb39927b6ddc3e40cd3a55b8ba4e.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The vertical asymptotes of the function are integer multiples of $\\pi $, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The domain of the function is all real numbers except the values where the asymptotes occur, i.e., $x=n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\nq-70669:\nLet's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934292/assets/8bc549c68a3bc5b7989ac1835e4bb58183066c7797264b8cccd4f3fd2c895056.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has no minimum or maximum value. $$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $n\\pi $ for any integer $n$, thus it is not defined for $x=-4\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The zeros of $y=cot⁡x$ occur at $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ for any integer $n.$ Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a zero of the function.\n\nq-103840:\nThe period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡x=cot⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x & =cot⁡(x+(-9)\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =cot⁡(x-9\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then $cot⁡(x-9\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nq-66675:\nThe period of $y=cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡x=cot⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-1$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡x=cot⁡(x+(-1)\\cdot \\pi )=cot⁡(x-\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $cot⁡x=\\sqrt{3},$ then $cot⁡(x-\\pi )=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}cot⁡(x-\\pi )-4 & =\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3})-4 \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =3-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-158055:\nFirst, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934292/assets/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-158064:\nFirst, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934292/assets/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 15) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6+7 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}+7 \\\\  & =3+7 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-36011:\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nq-66679:\nThe graph of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=(x-1)^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=(x-1)^{2}$  by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/f53a4f97166877085c90acc01e3f8d0c6ac7bb3f704bbfb792d747122d314013.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/b088c8ed1974a7c259dd54f85aec7a124579981971babfe8dee0313ca23b6458.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,3].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/b3af13782a01213bb09fdeef38fbeb3fbca3f41348036e6786b4efa74a0166b4.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nq-87606:\nIf we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1+5+1+2+3+6+1+2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1+25+1+4+9+36+1+4+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-42657:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$\n\nq-77451:\nTo find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938412/assets/52421034c2919620a0a9aae844db0eba2d396e5d6f1152d69bee40ff56c4d8ad.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$\n\nq-317285:\nTo plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938518/assets/2fe0fb3f0f5d302d39f6ad578148c4973618595452b59fea8a8bb2399ef92ddc.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{2}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938518/assets/98c622f4675812dcada993e4022398aac18f37cda76bbaf3ecbe6147c6e5bc97.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units down to give $y=-{2}^{x}-4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938518/assets/c5d06a922bc2fc4841bc33d0bd7dcc68244ce5ebacfd717aaa710044ec5c3caa.png)",
